{"kind":"assert","text":"LOG-1 (e79ccfaf, promise 80a0c32f), first answer, read at main 2216316e: a plain member session's `log` page is not filtered.\n\n- packages/scope/src/reads.ts:114 `#open`: a reader that the readers port allows gets `limit = null`. `#mine` (reads.ts:143) returns true for every row when `limit` is null or `whole`.\n- packages/scope/src/sessions.ts:303 `allows`: a member's session is allowed a read when its claims list it. `readsOf` (sessions.ts:341) gives every member `MEMBER_READS`, and that list names `log` (sessions.ts:121). So a member session at an application room reads `log` with `limit = null`, and every row stays.\n- A chained session at a register (reads.ts:128-131) gets `{ whole: true }`, so it is also unfiltered.\n- Only a signed read (no session) filters rows. checkSignedRead (signed-reads.ts:357) sets `whole` only at a register. Elsewhere `#mine` keeps the genesis, the key's own entries when `scoped`, and the entries whose chain leads to the key at `since` or later.\n\nOne more fact the handle needs: the page's `complete` and `next` come from the stored rows before `#mine` filters them (reads.ts:309-311). A filtered page can therefore say `complete: true` while it omits entries. The handle cannot tell a filtered page from a whole one by `complete` alone. It must find the gaps in `seq`. That is the contiguity check of scope item 2, so the check covers both cases.\n\nPlan: the handle reports `whole` when the seqs are contiguous from the cursor, `filtered` when they are not, and `below-floor` on `archived`. A replica takes only `whole` pages. The client documentation will say that a member session or a register session gets whole pages, and that a signed read may not. I build after NF-4 lands, in the client lane, unless you rule otherwise."}